From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 996: take the uplink from what the agent reports

Of two healthy agents, exactly one appeared in topology_link. Which one varied
from boot to boot, and it never corrected itself - measured across three cold
starts on 2026-07-29. Both agents were associated to the controller's backhaul
AP-MLD on two links each, both reachable, both carrying traffic.

topology_build_tree_from_assoc() places a node only when its upstream_bssid is
non-zero, and the sole writer of that field is find_backhaul_upstream(), called
while handling Link Metric messages. Captured from boot, that path shows why
the outcome is a coin flip:

  09:50:54  find_backhaul_upstream: No backhaul link found for
              3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b <-> 86:f7:56:41:01:d4
  09:53:04  find_backhaul_upstream: Found WiFi backhaul link via bBSS
              (topology not valid)

86:f7:56:41:01:d4 is the controller's own AP-MLD address. At 09:50 it was not
yet registered as a bBSS, so the lookup failed; two minutes later the same
lookup succeeded. Link Metric messages are rare - two in five minutes on this
mesh - and nothing retries a failed resolution, so whichever agent's message
lands in that early window keeps upstream_bssid all-zero for good.

The information does not have to come from there at all. The Backhaul STA MLD
Config TLV carries the AP-MLD the agent associated to, first-hand, and the
controller already parses it into bstamld->bssid and persists it - the row was
sitting in the database, correct, for both agents, while topology_link was
empty. Use it.

The tree builder already resolves a parent from an AP-MLD address
(find_node_with_mld_macaddr, patch 995), so the address is directly usable.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
diff -urN a/src/cntlr_map.c b/src/cntlr_map.c
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-29 11:55:37.014594460 +0200
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-29 11:55:37.015056059 +0200
@@ -4311,9 +4311,42 @@
 
 	if (flag & BSTA_MLD_CONF_BSTA_MLD_ADDR_VALID)
 		memcpy(mld->mld_macaddr, data + 1, 6);
-	if (flag & BSTA_MLD_CONF_AP_MLD_ADDR_VALID)
+	if (flag & BSTA_MLD_CONF_AP_MLD_ADDR_VALID) {
 		memcpy(mld->bssid, data + 7, 6);	/* AP-MLD assoc'd to */
 
+		/* This is the uplink, stated first-hand: the agent is telling us
+		 * which AP-MLD it associated to. Use it, because the only other
+		 * writer of node::upstream_bssid cannot be relied on.
+		 *
+		 * That writer is find_backhaul_upstream(), called while handling
+		 * Link Metric messages. Measured on a three-node mesh 2026-07-29,
+		 * captured from boot: those messages are rare - two in five
+		 * minutes - and the first attempt failed with
+		 *
+		 *   find_backhaul_upstream: No backhaul link found for
+		 *     3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b <-> 86:f7:56:41:01:d4
+		 *
+		 * because 86:f7:56:41:01:d4 is the controller's own AP-MLD
+		 * address, which was not yet registered as a bBSS at that moment.
+		 * Two minutes later the same lookup succeeded. Nothing retries the
+		 * failed one, so whichever agent's message lands in that early
+		 * window keeps upstream_bssid all-zero forever - and
+		 * topology_build_tree_from_assoc() skips exactly the nodes whose
+		 * upstream_bssid is zero.
+		 *
+		 * The visible result was that of two healthy agents exactly one
+		 * appeared in topology_link, which one varied from boot to boot,
+		 * and it never corrected itself. Both were associated, both
+		 * carried traffic on both links.
+		 *
+		 * The tree builder already resolves a parent given an AP-MLD
+		 * address (find_node_with_mld_macaddr), so this address is
+		 * directly usable as it stands.
+		 */
+		if (!hwaddr_is_zero(mld->bssid))
+			memcpy(n->upstream_bssid, mld->bssid, 6);
+	}
+
 	mld->str_enabled   = !!(flag2 & BSTA_MLD_CONFIG_STR);
 	mld->nstr_enabled  = !!(flag2 & BSTA_MLD_CONFIG_NSTR);
 	mld->emlsr_enabled = !!(flag2 & BSTA_MLD_CONFIG_EMLSR);
